    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the smallest number divisible by integers 1 through 9, we need to find the least common multiple (LCM) of these numbers. The LCM of a set of integers is the smallest positive integer that is divisible by each of the integers in the set.\n\nWe can use the prime factorization method to find the LCM. We first find the prime factorization of each number:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 & = 1 \\\\\n2 & = 2 \\\\\n3 & = 3 \\\\\n4 & = 2^2 \\\\\n5 & = 5 \\\\\n6 & = 2 \\cdot 3 \\\\\n7 & = 7 \\\\\n8 & = 2^3 \\\\\n9 & = 3^2 \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nNext, we take the highest power of each prime that appears in the factorization of any of the numbers:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n2^3 & \\quad (2^3 \\text{ from } 8) \\\\\n3^2 & \\quad (3^2 \\text{ from } 9) \\\\\n5 & \\quad (5 \\text{ from } 5) \\\\\n7 & \\quad (7 \\text{ from } 7) \\\\\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThe LCM is the product of these highest powers:\n\n\\[\n2^3 \\cdot 3^2 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 7 = 8 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 7\n\\]\n\nNow we compute this product step-by-step:\n\n\\[\n8 \\cdot 9 = 72\n\\]\n\\[\n72 \\cdot 5 = 360\n\\]\n\\[\n360 \\cdot 7 = 2520\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the smallest number divisible by integers 1 through 9 is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2520}\n\\]",
